  "name": "24 HR URINARY PHOSPHORUS",
  "code": "1004",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>A 24-hour urinary phosphorus test measures the total amount of phosphorus excreted in the urine over a 24-hour period and is primarily used to evaluate phosphorus metabolism, renal tubular function, and calcium-phosphorus balance.</p>\n<div>High urinary phosphorus excretion may indicate:</div>\n<div>&bull; High dietary phosphorus intake</div>\n<div>&bull; Hyperparathyroidism (overactive parathyroid gland increasing phosphorus excretion)</div>\n<div>&bull; Proximal renal tubular defects (e.g., Fanconi syndrome)</div>\n<div>&bull; Extracellular volume expansion or low potassium intake</div>\n<div>&bull; Conditions causing phosphorus wasting or impaired reabsorption</div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n<div>Low urinary phosphorus excretion may indicate:</div>\n<div>&bull; Low dietary phosphorus intake</div>\n<div>&bull; Hypoparathyroidism (low parathyroid hormone reducing phosphorus excretion)</div>\n<div>&bull; Impaired renal phosphorus excretion due to kidney dysfunction</div>\n<div>&bull; Vitamin D deficiency or malabsorption reducing intestinal phosphorus absorption</div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n<div>Because urinary phosphorus excretion is regulated mainly by proximal tubule reabsorption influenced by hormones and dietary intake, results must be interpreted alongside serum phosphorus levels and clinical context (e.g., kidney function, parathyroid status)</div>",
